      "blocks": {
        "q1": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Do I need an apostille for Qatar?",
            "answer": "<p>No, an apostille alone isn't enough. Qatar isn't part of the Hague Apostille Convention, so your documents go through full legalisation: verification at home, your foreign ministry, the Qatar embassy in your country, then Qatar's Ministry of Foreign Affairs.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q2": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Do South African teachers need a SAQA evaluation for Qatar?",
            "answer": "<p>What you need is a SAQA verification letter for your degree (Umalusi for matric). DIRCO uses it before issuing a Certificate of Authentication. We found no Qatari requirement for a separate SAQA evaluation.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q3": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Can I teach in Qatar with an online degree?",
            "answer": "<p>Be careful. The Qatar Embassy in London does not attest distance or online certificates, except postgraduate MSc and PhD degrees. Check with your school and the embassy before you start attestation.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q4": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Do I need a Qatari teaching licence before I arrive?",
            "answer": "<p>There's no sign of that. The Ministry of Education's Professional Licence is built on years of experience, training hours and annual evaluations in Qatar, so teachers earn it once they are working there.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q5": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Who handles my residence permit and Qatar ID?",
            "answer": "<p>Your school sponsors you, and its PRO usually guides you through it. The medical, fingerprints and residence permit must be completed within 30 days of arrival, and you get your Qatar ID after that.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q6": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Can I bring my family to Qatar?",
            "answer": "<p>Yes, if you meet the rules. In the private sector you need a salary of QR10,000, or QR6,000 with family housing from your employer, plus health insurance for your family and school places for children aged 6 to 18.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q7": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Can my spouse work in Qatar?",
            "answer": "<p>Yes, with a work permit from the Ministry of Labour. A spouse who wants to teach must meet the same qualification rules as any other teacher.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q8": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Can I change schools in Qatar?",
            "answer": "<p>Yes. Since 2020 most workers no longer need a no-objection certificate or an exit permit. Notice is one month if you have worked up to two years, and two months after that.</p>"
          }
        }
      },
